        "problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}+2x?$",
        "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1),(-2,2),$ and $(2,3).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=(-2)^{2}+2(-2)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,2)}=(-2)^{2}+2(-2)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,3)}=(2)^{2}+2(2)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/4b6ef4f2b56acf2d09a07d73c19f49f5b1c6b9eee17caa382a5c9924cbb0ea5f.png)",
